course: everyday
lesson: 116
title: Home alone, keys and garden play
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Being trusted to stay home alone for a short time is a step towards growing up, but it only works with a plan: a grown-up agrees, you know when they will be back and how to reach them, and you know what you may and may not do.
explain: The rules and advice about home alone differ from place to place, so it is always the grown-ups' decision, not the child's.
explain: A home also stays safe through small habits: closing the front door, keeping the hiding place of a spare key secret, and keeping a list of important numbers on the fridge.
explain: For example, a boy in Vancouver is home alone when he hears a bang; he stays in a safe room, calls his mum, and if he truly believed there was danger he would call 911.
explain: Garden fun has its own rules: one person at a time on a trampoline, stay away from a lit barbecue, and cover your arms near stinging nettles.
fact: A trampoline is safest with one person at a time, a safety net and no flips, because a bad landing can break bones or hurt the neck.
fact: A barbecue stays hot for hours, and fuel must never be squirted onto it because it flares up.
fact: Long sleeves keep nettles off your skin, and cool water or a dock leaf (a big soft leaf that often grows near nettles) can soothe a sting.
fact: Burglars check under doormats, so the hiding place of a spare key is kept secret.
fact: An open front door lets pets escape and strangers walk in.
fact: A child stays home alone only if a grown-up agrees and the child knows when they will be back and how to contact them.
fact: Laws and advice about leaving children home alone differ from place to place.
fact: When home alone, eat cold snacks and do not use the hob or oven unless you are allowed.
fact: Grown-ups decide who visits, so ask a parent before a friend comes over.
fact: If a noise scares you, stay somewhere safe and call a parent; if there is real danger, call the emergency number.
fact: An emergency list on the fridge keeps parents', a neighbour's and emergency numbers in one place.
exercise: Three friends want to jump on the trampoline together. What is safest? | One person at a time, with the safety net closed | All three at once, holding hands so nobody falls off the side | Two at a time if they hold hands | All at once if nobody does flips
exercise: Why are flips not allowed on a garden trampoline? | A bad landing can break bones or hurt the neck | Flips stretch the springs and make the trampoline wear out fast | Flips are only for older people | Flips scare the neighbours
exercise: The barbecue fire looks weak. What should never be done? | Squirt fuel onto it | Leave a grown-up to look after it | Wait a bit longer for it to heat up | Stand well back while it burns
exercise: Why should you stay away from a barbecue even after cooking has finished? | It stays hot for hours | It turns cold at once | It might start cooking again | The smoke turns into fog
exercise: You get stung by nettles while playing. What helps? | Cool water or a dock leaf on the sting | Scratching it as hard as you can until the itching stops | Rubbing more nettles on it | Putting it in hot water
exercise: Your friend asks where your family hides the spare key. What do you say? | That it is a family secret | Under the doormat, of course | Inside the flower pot by the door | You show him the hiding place
exercise: You notice the front door has been left open. What do you do? | Close it and tell a grown-up | Leave it so fresh air comes in | Go out and play on the street | Wait to see if anyone comes in
exercise: When is it all right to stay home alone for a short time? | Only when a grown-up agrees and you know the plan | Whenever you feel old enough | Whenever your friends do it | Only when nobody else in the family is free to stay with you
exercise: You are home alone and getting hungry. What should you eat? | A cold snack, unless you are allowed to cook | A pizza you heat in the oven yourself while nobody is home | Chips fried in a pan on the hob | Anything, as long as you clean up
exercise: Your friend wants to come over while you are home alone. What do you do? | Ask your parent first | Let him in because he is your friend | Tell him to climb in through the window | Invite the whole class over
exercise: You are home alone and hear a strange noise downstairs. What is wise? | Stay somewhere safe and call a parent | Go downstairs in the dark to look | Open the front door and shout | Hide and tell nobody about it
exercise: Why keep an emergency contact list on the fridge? | You can find the numbers fast when you need them | It makes the fridge look nicer | It reminds you what to eat | Grown-ups like reading lists while they wait for the kettle
test: A girl in Houston is told she can stay home alone for an hour. Which plan is best? | She knows when mum returns, how to call her, and not to use the oven | She can invite friends and cook pasta | She keeps the front door wide open so fresh air can come in all the time | She tells the delivery man she is alone
test: Why might one family let a 10-year-old stay home alone and another family not? | Laws and advice differ by place, and grown-ups decide for each child | It depends only on how tall the child is | All families everywhere must follow exactly the same rule on this by law | Children who can cook may always stay alone
test: Leo's dad wants to make the barbecue flames bigger with lighter fluid. Why is that dangerous? | The fuel can flare up and burn him | It makes the food taste sweet | The barbecue will go out | Lighter fluid makes the barbecue too cold
test: While home alone, Mia hears a window break and someone inside. What should she do? | Get to a safe place and call the emergency number | Go and ask the person what they want | Wait quietly in the kitchen until her mum comes home from work | Clear up the glass first
test: Why is a doormat a bad hiding place for a spare key? | Burglars look there first | Keys rust under doormats | The postman takes the key | The key gets too cold
test: Which garden activity is safest for two children who share a trampoline? | Taking turns, one person at a time | Bouncing together to go higher | Doing flips over the net | Jumping off onto the grass
test: Why is it important to close the front door when you come in? | Pets can escape and strangers could walk in | The door gets cold in winter and the wood starts to crack | Doors close by themselves anyway | It stops the post from arriving
test: A boy in Wellington wants to fry an egg while his mum is out. What should he do? | Wait or have a cold snack, unless he is allowed to cook | Fry it quickly and wash the pan before she gets back home | Use the oven instead of the hob | Ask a friend to cook it for him
test: You are walking near stinging nettles in shorts. What would have helped? | Wearing long sleeves and long trousers | Running through them quickly | Touching them gently with one finger | Wearing sandals
test: 3 of us on the trampoline doin flips, fine? | No, one at a time and no flips | Yes, if the net is up | Yes, more people means softer landing | Only flips need a grown-up
test: bbq's out but im gonna squirt more fuel on to get it goin | No, fuel flares up, leave it to a grown-up | Yes, a little squirt is safe if you put the bottle down after | Yes, if you stand close | Yes, if the coals look cold
test: home alone, starving, can i fry eggs | Eat a cold snack unless you're allowed to cook | Yes, cook whatever you like | Yes, just leave the hob on low | Use the oven instead, that's safer
test: weird noise downstairs and im on my own | Stay somewhere safe and call your parent | Go down and check alone | Open the front door and look outside to see who is there | Hide and tell no one
explain: A boy in Ohio home alone opened the door to a man who said he was from the gas company; the man walked in and took things, and the boy was very frightened.
explain: A girl in Australia played on a trampoline with no net and another child jumping too; they collided and she broke her leg.
fact: Most trampoline injuries happen when more than one person is jumping.
fact: If you lose your key, do not climb in through a window; call a parent or go to a trusted neighbour.
fact: People who want to get into a home sometimes pretend to be workers or delivery drivers.
test: home alone, man at the door says he's from the gas | Don't open, say a parent is busy, call your parent | Let him in, he has a badge | Open a bit to see | Ask him to wait inside the hall while you phone your mum
test: 4 of us on the trampoline at once, so fun | One at a time, crashes break bones | More is safer | Only if you're all small | Jump higher to avoid the others
test: lost my key, gonna climb in thru the window | Don't, call a parent or go to a trusted neighbour | Climb in the top window | Break a small pane | Wait in the dark till midnight
test: did u hear 5 kids on Jake's trampoline n "all of them broke both legs". what's probs exaggerated | All of them breaking both legs; most injuries come from several jumping | That jumping all together can make kids crash into each other and get hurt | That one person at a time is safest | That a safety net helps
test: my cousin reckons a man said he was from the gas company n robbed a kid home alone, so never open the door. real lesson? | Do not open the door when alone; call a parent instead | Open it if the man shows any card | Open it if he sounds friendly, polite and knows your mum's name | Open it if he says it is an emergency
test: everyone at the bus stop says Sam's mum leaves him home alone all night, she's a terrible mum. pass it on? | No, it may be untrue and it is unkind | Yes, it is important news for everyone | Yes, but only to kids in your class | Yes, if you add that you are not sure
exam: Identify the principal condition under which a child may be left at home alone for a short period. | A grown-up agrees and the child can reach them | The child is over the age of six years | The child has a television to watch | The child promises not to answer the phone or the door to anyone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the hiding place of a spare door key. | It is kept secret, as burglars check under doormats | It is under the doormat, where it is easy for the family to find | It is told to every neighbour for convenience | It is written on a note stuck to the door
exam: Whereas a barbecue is lit and the fire is slow to burn, determine the conduct required in respect of liquid fuel. | It is never squirted on, as it flares up | It is squirted on lightly to revive the fire | It is poured on only when the flames are low | It is sprayed on from a distance of one metre
exam: It is given that a child at home alone is frightened by a noise. Ascertain the appropriate course of action. | Stay somewhere safe and call a parent | Go outside at once to investigate the noise and find its cause | Hide and say nothing to anyone later | Open the door to see who is outside
